    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d896">

                <pb facs="0967" n="865" sameAs="frus:frus1919v01/pg865"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">818.6363/2</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at San José</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Lee</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                            Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >San José</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1913-11-22"><hi rendition="#italic">November 22,
                            1913</hi>.</date><lb/>[<hi rendition="#italic">Received December
                        4.</hi>]</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to invite reference to my
                    unnumbered despatch of the 4th instant entitled “Trade Notes”,<anchor n="33"
                        xml:id="d896-f1"/> and report that the National Congress of Costa Rica on
                    November 17th, 1913, in secret session, disapproved the proposed contract or
                    concession granting <pb facs="0968" n="866" xml:id="pg866"/> to Messrs. S.
                    Pearson &amp; Son of London rights to explore and develop possible oil fields in
                    Costa Rica.</p>
                <p>The contract referred to is given in full in <hi rendition="#italic">La Guceta
                        Oficial</hi> of November 4th, 1913, copies of which accompanied my despatch
                    of the 4th instant.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Samuel T. Lee</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d896-f1" n="33" xml:id="d896-f1-content">
                <p>Not found in Department files; for text, see U.S. Department of Commerce, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Daily Consular and Trade Reports</hi>, No. 274, Nov. 22,
                    1913, p. 991. It contains the statement: “There is at present a proposition
                    before Congress to grant a concession to S. Pearson &amp; Son, of London, giving
                    rights to explore and develop possible oil fields in Costa Rica.”</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
